Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book by Margery Williams
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A warm, painterly storybook world — soft brushed textures, gaslight gold. THE BOY, six years old, tousled and pajama-clad, digs through a bulging Christmas stocking. Nuts, an orange, a toy engine, a clockwork mouse tumble out. Wedged in the top, a sprig of holly between his paws, sits THE RABBIT — fat and bunchy velveteen, spotted brown and white, pink sateen ears, thread whiskers.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

THE BOY hugs the Rabbit close, delighted.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him. Then Aunts and Uncles came to dinner, and in the rustling of tissue paper and the unwrapping of new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

Wrapping paper swallows the little Rabbit on the floor as small feet hurry past.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. THE RABBIT sits shelved among gleaming, self-important toys — a brass clockwork boat with peeling paint, a wind-up mouse, TIMOTHY the jointed wooden lion.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was naturally shy, and being only made of velveteen, some of the more expensive toys quite snubbed him. The mechanical toys were very superior, and pretended they were real.

The toys turn their backs on him. THE RABBIT droops.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

Near the fender lies THE SKIN HORSE — ancient, bald in patches, seams showing, his tail thinned to a few threads. THE RABBIT settles beside him.

THE RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

THE SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

THE RABBIT
Does it hurt?

THE SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

THE RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

THE SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

THE RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

THE SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. But once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

THE RABBIT sighs, torn between longing and dread.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA sweeps through, brisk and impatient, hunting for a lost china dog. She spots the toy cupboard standing open and swoops.

NANA
Here — take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags THE RABBIT out by one ear and drops him into THE BOY's arms.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed. And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy — so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

THE BOY burrows the Rabbit under the blankets, whispering, laughing. The pink on the Rabbit's nose has worn pale.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. THE BOY wheels THE RABBIT through the flowerbeds in a wheelbarrow, builds him a fairy hut of raspberry canes. Dusk falls; the lawn empties. THE RABBIT lies alone in wet grass, forgotten, until NANA arrives with a candle, grumbling, and rubs him down with her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

THE BOY sits up in bed, arms out.

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

Close on the Rabbit's button eyes — worn dull, yet lit now with something like wisdom.

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Summer. THE RABBIT lies alone in a bracken nest, watching ants cross his paws. Two wild rabbits — sleek, brand-new, seamless — creep out of the ferns. One, BRAMBLE, noses close.

BRAMBLE
Why don't you get up and play with us?

THE RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

BRAMBLE
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

Bramble hops sideways onto his hind legs.

BRAMBLE (CONT'D)
Can you hop on your hind legs?

THE RABBIT sits frozen — he has no hind legs at all, his body one seamless velvet cushion.

THE RABBIT
I don't want to!

BRAMBLE
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

THE RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs — I am sitting on them!

Bramble whirls in a mocking dance. Then stops, noses close — and flattens his ears, recoiling.

BRAMBLE
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

THE RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps — THE BOY runs past. A flash of white tails, and the wild rabbits vanish into the bracken.

THE RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only the ants answer, running to and fro. The bracken sways, empty.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Months later. THE RABBIT — hairless in patches, one eye loose, shape nearly gone — lies tangled in the sheets. THE BOY, flushed and glassy-eyed, mutters in fevered sleep. THE RABBIT presses close, unmoving, hidden beneath the blankets whenever footsteps pass.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Strange people came and went, and a light burned all night, and through it all the little Velveteen Rabbit lay there, hidden from sight, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away — and he knew that the Boy needed him.

Time passes — the fever breaks. THE BOY sits up, color returned, a picture-book in his lap, THE RABBIT tucked at his side.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Later. THE BOY, wrapped in a shawl, is carried out toward a sunlit balcony. THE DOCTOR speaks with NANA over a heap of books and toys pulled from the bed.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

THE DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs — burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

THE RABBIT lies half-buried in the bedclothes, listening, his head just peeping out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
"Hurrah!" thought the little Rabbit. "To-morrow we shall go to the seaside!" He did not understand.

EXT. GARDEN - BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

THE RABBIT lies stuffed in a burlap sack among old picture-books and rubbish, left for the gardener's bonfire. He wriggles his head through the loose ties and looks out at the raspberry thicket, silvered by moonlight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden — how happy they were — and a great sadness came over him. Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real, if it all ended like this?

A single tear slides down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the earth.

Where it lands, a slender green shoot pushes up — leaves like emeralds, a blossom like a golden cup. It opens. THE FAIRY steps out, dressed in pearl and dew, flowers threaded through her hair.

THE FAIRY
Little Rabbit — don't you know who I am? I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out, and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me, and turn them into Real.

THE RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

THE FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She gathers him into her arms and kisses his damp velvet nose.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

The Fairy carries THE RABBIT into moonlit bracken shining like frosted silver. In a glade, wild rabbits dance with their own shadows — they freeze, forming a ring, staring at her.

THE FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him, and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land — for he is going to live with you for ever and ever.

She sets him down on the grass and kisses him once more.

THE FAIRY (CONT'D)
Run and play, little Rabbit.

THE RABBIT sits frozen, ashamed of his one-piece body, afraid to move — until something tickles his nose. Without thinking, he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He stops. Looks down. Soft brown fur. Real hind legs. Whiskers brushing the grass.

He leaps — once, twice — springing and whirling across the turf with the others, wild with the joy of it. When at last he turns to find the Fairy, she is gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

The following Spring. THE BOY, a little taller now, plays at the edge of the trees. Two rabbits creep out from the bracken to watch him — one brown all over, the other marked faintly beneath his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still showed through.

THE BOY studies the second rabbit — the familiar set of the nose, the round dark eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
"Why," the Boy thought to himself, "he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!" But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

THE RABBIT holds the Boy's gaze one long moment — then turns, and disappears into the bracken with the other.

FADE OUT.

THE END
